# Federal Program Performance Command Center

I rebuilt this project as a case study in one of the most persistent modernization problems: leadership can have access to a large amount of program data and still lack a reliable picture of program health.

> All agencies, programs, users, financial values, schedules, and performance records in this project are fictional.

## Challenge

A fictional federal portfolio is managed across spreadsheets, status slides, SharePoint lists, email updates, and legacy systems. Consequently, program managers spend significant time reconciling information before they can analyze it, while leadership receives information after the reporting cycle rather than at the point when intervention has the greatest value.

The core challenges are fragmented sources, inconsistent measures, manual reporting, duplicate/incomplete records, delayed milestone visibility, limited cost and schedule forecasting, and weak linkage between risks, issues, corrective actions, and executive decisions.

## Solution

I designed a centralized Program Performance Command Center that connects program, project, milestone, funding, risk, issue, corrective-action, and status information within a governed analytical model. Power Apps supports operational updates, Power Automate standardizes intake and alerts, and Power BI converts the underlying records into leadership indicators and forward-looking forecasts.

## Challenge-to-Solution Alignment

| Challenge | Solution I Designed |
|---|---|
| Program information is fragmented | Centralized relational program-performance model |
| Reports require manual reconciliation | Automated ingestion, validation, and reporting pipeline |
| Leadership cannot see deteriorating programs | Evidence-based Green/Amber/Red health logic |
| Milestones are identified late | Due-date logic and 30/60/90-day forecasting |
| Risks/issues are separated from performance | Linked risk, issue, milestone, and corrective-action records |
